  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1206 PM CDT Mon Jul 13 2026\n\nThe main focus Tuesday night through Thursday will be on the \npotential for heavy rainfall and flooding/flash flooding. On \nTuesday strong upper-level ridging will be in place, along with a\nseries of upper-level disturbances and a stout influx of tropical\nmoisture across the area. This has led model guidance to show\nincreased chances for highly efficient showers and thunderstorms\nthat can produce moderate to heavy rainfall across much of the\narea through Thursday. The airmass will be very moist (PWAT - \nprecipitable water values of 1.5 to 2.5+ inches) and uncapped, \nand will likely have leftover outflow boundaries from the\nconvection we experience today. A Flood Watch has been issued for\nthe Northern Edwards Plateau, Northwest Hill Country, and \nsouthern portions of both the Concho Valley and Heartland from 10\nPM CDT this evening through early Thursday evening to cover the \nrisk of Flash Flooding potential. \n\nAn upper low is forecast to move west-northwest along or just \nsouth of I-10 Tuesday night and Wednesday, to near or just north \nof the Big Bend area by Thursday. This track and placement would \nprovide more favorable lift with better upper support over our \narea, and can result in more organized/widespread convection over\nour southern and central counties for Wednesday. Models are also\nshowing a weak low-level jet developing Wednesday night and a \nlittle stronger low-level jet Thursday night. This will help to \nprovide an influx of low-level moisture, and precipitable water \nvalues are forecast to be 1.5 to 2.5+ inches. With the above \nmentioned scenario and increased cloud cover, daytime temperatures\nTuesday through Thursday should be limited to mostly the 80s for \nhighs. The upper low is progged to move slowly west-southwest \ninto far northern Mexico by Friday night into Saturday. With a \nslower progression now indicated, the chance of showers and \nthunderstorms will linger in at least our western counties on \nFriday. The weekend will trend warmer and drier with gradually \nbuilding heights aloft. Medium range models differ on \nplacement/repositioning of an upper level ridge, and its proximity\nto our area. The forecast at this time has highs ranging from the\nupper 80s/lower 90s in our southern counties, to the mid 90s in \nthe Big Country north of I-20 Saturday, and mid to upper 90s \nSunday.",
